WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:04.800
Materials make
distinctive sounds

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.521
when you hit and scratch them.

00:00:06.521 --> 00:00:10.940
[HITTING SOUNDS]

00:00:12.336 --> 00:00:14.460
These sounds can tell us
how objects behave when we

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:15.966
physically interact with them.

00:00:15.966 --> 00:00:17.340
They can convey
whether an object

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:20.720
is hard or soft, smooth or
rough, and rigid or deformable.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.372
In this work, we consider the
material understanding task

00:00:23.372 --> 00:00:24.830
of predicting what
sounds something

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:26.400
will make when you hit it.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.230
We give an algorithm
a silent video

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.540
of someone striking
things with a drumstick.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.690
We ask it to produce a plausible
soundtrack to go along with it.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:35.520
To perform this task
well, the algorithm

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.592
has to recognize the
materials that are being hit

00:00:37.592 --> 00:00:39.300
and the actions that
are being performed.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.670
But we don't explicitly
tell it about these things.

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:44.430
Instead, it has to learn about
them by recognizing patterns

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:46.950
in the raw audio visual signal.

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.890
Our algorithm takes a
video sequence as input

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:53.280
and it predicts a corresponding
sequence of sound features.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.380
After a network
predicts sound features,

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.690
we synthesize a waveform
by matching these features

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.450
to a database of impact sounds
and transferring the best

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:01.380
matches.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:04.860
Here are some soundtracks
that our algorithm produced.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:09.315
[HITTING SOUNDS]

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:18.200
To help understand
what our model's doing,

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.270
we can look at the video
clips in our database

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:22.070
that it's transferring
audio from.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:23.690
These are the audio
clips whose sound

00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:27.603
features are most similar to
those predicted by our model.

00:01:27.603 --> 00:01:31.522
[SCRATCHING SOUNDS]

00:01:32.022 --> 00:01:35.950
[HITTING SOUNDS]

00:01:36.932 --> 00:01:41.351
[BANGING SOUNDS]

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:49.850
Instead of synthesizing a
waveform by transferring sounds

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:52.700
from a database, we can directly
invert the sound features

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:54.830
with a simple parametric
synthesis method.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:56.746
This can help us
understand what information

00:01:56.746 --> 00:01:58.370
is contained in the
sound features that

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:00.266
are model predicted.

00:02:00.266 --> 00:02:04.748
[BANGING SOUNDS]

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:12.930
Our algorithm can get confused
when the motion of a drumstick

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:16.582
resembles a hit but it does
not actually strike a surface.

00:02:16.582 --> 00:02:19.691
[BANGING SOUNDS]

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:22.530
We conducted a human study
where we asked people

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:24.870
to distinguish real impact
sounds from sounds that

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.609
were synthesized by our model.

00:02:26.609 --> 00:02:28.900
We found that our sound
predictions fooled participants

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.050
more often than those
of baseline methods,

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:32.940
including simple
image matching models.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:39.290
[SCRATCHING AND BANGING SOUNDS]

